{
  "image_name": "0b2f69da060bac86bde6b5907dd42e44.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the SNARE-mediated pathway by which a transport vesicle docks and fuses with a target organelle membrane, then resets the fusion machinery for reuse. A vesicle carrying v-SNARE proteins approaches a membrane containing t-SNAREs and SNAPs. These proteins assemble into a trans-SNARE complex that bridges the two membranes and appears to pull them tightly together. As this complex “zippers” up, the membranes bend inward and merge, leading to vesicle fusion and cargo delivery.\n\nAfter fusion, the SNARE proteins remain associated on the same membrane and are then disassembled by the factors α-SNAP and NSF, shown binding to the post-fusion complex. This recycling step frees the SNARE components so they can be used again in another round of vesicle trafficking.\n\nThe lower inset gives a closer molecular view of the bridging complex across the two lipid bilayers. It identifies example v-SNAREs, VAMP3 and VAMP4, and target-membrane components including STX6 and STX16, with SNAPs contributing to the bundle. Overall, the diagram emphasizes the cyclical sequence of docking, membrane fusion, complex disassembly, and SNARE recycling.",
